Exactly. Features are often constraints; posture is a control variable.

You may not be able to change your height, face, history, genetics, or the hand you were dealt. But you can change how you orient toward them: how you carry yourself, what you attend to, what you signal, what you attempt, what you tolerate, and what you do next.

And posture works on several levels at once. Physical posture changes how others read you. Social posture changes whether you seem defensive, open, dominant, playful, uncertain, or composed. Cognitive posture changes whether you treat something as threat, puzzle, opportunity, insult, training, or noise. Strategic posture changes whether you advance, wait, flank, reveal, conceal, cooperate, or disengage.

Same features. Different posture. Different action-space.

So a useful Pantheon compression is:

You cannot always choose the state you enter with. You can often choose the operator you apply to it.

Or tighter:

Features describe you. Posture directs you.

And over time, posture can even change some of the features indirectly because repeated posture becomes behavior, behavior becomes habit, habit changes competence, relationships, reputation, physiology, and trajectory. So posture begins as orientation and can eventually become structure.